{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "186",
    "case_summary": "A 70-year-old Japanese man presented with dysphagia and was diagnosed with T3N0M0 esophageal squamous cell carcinoma. His medical history included type 2 diabetes mellitus (HbA1c 6.6%), percutaneous coronary intervention, and radiotherapy for laryngeal cancer. He underwent two courses of 5-fluorouracil/docetaxel/cisplatin and then thoracoscopic esophagectomy with retrosternal gastric tube reconstruction and cervical esophagogastrostomy.\n\nOn postoperative day 4, cervical wound erythema with pus and saliva discharge appeared; drainage and intravenous tazobactam/piperacillin were initiated. Despite nutritional support and improved albumin levels, a refractory anastomotic fistula persisted, and Enterobacter cloacae was cultured from the wound. On day 60, salvage reconstruction with a free jejunal autograft and pectoralis major muscle flap coverage was performed.\n\nFifteen days after reconstruction, he developed worsening low back pain and fever (38.2 °C). Examination revealed lumbar spinal tenderness and bilateral leg weakness without sensory loss. Laboratory tests showed a white blood cell count of 9200/µL and C-reactive protein of 9.86 mg/dL. Chest radiography and abdominal ultrasound were unremarkable. Blood cultures grew Enterobacter cloacae. MRI of the lumbar spine demonstrated decreased T1 signal and increased T2 and STIR signal at L4–L5.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. pyogenic spondylodiscitis\nB. Hematogenous spread most likely\nC. Direct infiltration excluded\nD. Contiguous spread unlikely\nE. Metastatic spinal tumor considered and excluded\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
